# ffmpeg configure flags, shared by every build of it Beacon ships — the
# Dockerfile's ffmpeg-builder stage and scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh. Comments and
# blank lines are stripped, everything else is passed to ./configure as-is.
# Anything platform-specific (how to link, where to find libraries) stays with
# the build that needs it; only what the app itself requires lives here.
#
# connect only ever transcodes audio — video is always explicitly disabled
# (-vn). Starting from nothing and adding back what is actually reachable
# gets a ~130MB distribution ffmpeg down to ~10MB with no runtime
# dependencies at all.
#
# **A name in this file that ffmpeg doesn't know is not an error.** configure
# builds one alternation out of a whole comma list and only warns when
# *nothing* in it matched, so four correct names hide a fifth that is wrong
# and the build succeeds with a component quietly missing. It has cost this
# project several releases (a missing `volume` filter meant every track with
# ReplayGain failed in the Docker image; `--enable-parser=mp3` did nothing
# for a year because the parser is called `mpegaudio`). That is what
# build/ffmpeg/required-components and scripts/verify-ffmpeg.sh exist for:
# every build is checked against what the code can actually ask for, and a
# name that fell through fails the build instead of a listener's track.
--disable-everything
--disable-doc
--disable-debug
--disable-avdevice
--disable-swscale

# Input is http(s) URLs (a media server's stream URL, or a radio station's),
# plus the local file paths the test tone and analysis paths use and the
# pipe the radio relay feeds a station's bytes through. tcp is what http
# rides on, tls what https adds.
#
# udp is in the list for the linker, not for us: nothing here ever speaks
# it, but ffmpeg's TLS code carries DTLS with it and calls into the udp
# protocol unconditionally, so dropping it fails the build outright with
# "undefined reference to ff_udp_get_last_recv_addr". Tried and measured,
# 2026-09-08 — it is not a leftover.
--enable-protocol=file,http,https,tls,tcp,udp,pipe
--enable-openssl

# The demuxer and decoder lists are the library side of the rule: a library
# holds whatever the person put in it, and every format the three media
# servers index has to at least be decodable — the app's own "Original"
# setting hands connect anything the browser can't play (see
# streamQuality.ts's LOSSLESS set) expecting a lossless FLAC re-wrap back.
# A missing decoder there is a track that simply refuses to play.
#
# The two lists have to be read together, and their names differ from what
# `ffmpeg -demuxers` prints for two of them: Shorten's demuxer configures as
# `shorten` and reports as `shn`, and DSDIFF (.dff) has no demuxer of its
# own — it is part of `iff`. required-components carries the reported names.
--enable-demuxer=mp3,flac,ogg,wav,aac,mov,matroska,asf,ape,aiff,wv,dsf,iff,mpc,mpc8,tta,shorten,loas
--enable-decoder=mp3,mp3float,mp2,mp2float,flac,vorbis,opus,aac,aac_latm,alac,ape,wavpack,tta,shorten,mpc7,mpc8,wmav1,wmav2,wmapro,wmalossless,dsd_lsbf,dsd_msbf,dsd_lsbf_planar,dsd_msbf_planar,pcm_s8,pcm_u8,pcm_s16le,pcm_s16be,pcm_s24le,pcm_s24be,pcm_s32le,pcm_s32be,pcm_f32le,pcm_f32be,pcm_f64le,pcm_f64be

# The PCM entries above are every depth *and byte order* a WAV or AIFF can
# carry. AIFF is big-endian, which is the half that was missing: a 32-bit
# AIFF probed perfectly and then failed at every step after it with "no
# decoder found for pcm_s32be", because s16be and s24be were listed and
# s32be was not.
#
# WMA needs three decoders for what looks like one format — wmav1/wmav2 for
# ordinary WMA, wmapro and wmalossless for the other two the asf demuxer
# happily hands over.
#
# `mpegaudio` covers MPEG layers 1-3, so it is the parser mp3 needs; `mp3`
# is not a parser name at all. aac_latm is paired with the `loas` demuxer:
# without it the decoder is unreachable and an AAC+ station in LOAS framing
# has nothing to open it.
--enable-parser=mpegaudio,aac,aac_latm,flac,opus,vorbis

# The encoder list has to cover every format the app can actually ask for,
# which is not the same as every format it usually uses. `aac` is in it
# because Beacon offers AAC as a cast quality (streamQuality.ts's
# CAST_FORMATS, reached through lossy_encode_args()); without it that setting
# failed with "Unknown encoder 'aac'" in the Docker image while working fine
# on a desktop install with a distribution ffmpeg. Adding a format to
# CAST_FORMATS or LOCAL_FORMATS means adding its encoder (and muxer) here
# too — and to required-components, which is what turns forgetting into a
# failed build.
#
# core/audio_analysis.py's live FFT visualizer and core/waveform.py's
# seek-bar peaks both decode to raw PCM via "-f s16le", which needs BOTH the
# pcm_s16le muxer and the pcm_s16le encoder — ffmpeg needs a registered
# encoder component to write into that container even though "encoding" raw
# PCM is a passthrough. Missing the muxer is "Unknown output format", missing
# the encoder is "Automatic encoder selection failed ... probably disabled".
# The configure-time muxer name carries a pcm_ prefix the runtime `-f` name
# doesn't, which is the second name in here that does not match what the
# binary reports.
#
# The muxers are also what the copy tier needs: resolve_output_format()
# prefers stream-copying a source codec straight through, and a copy needs an
# output container rather than an encoder. Opus is deliberately not in that
# tier despite ffmpeg supporting an opus-in-ogg copy — a real Sonos accepts
# the URI and produces no audio for it — so the ogg muxer is here for Vorbis.
# flac is also the universal re-encode target for other lossless sources
# (alac, WAV/AIFF PCM, ape, WavPack, DSD, ...) and needs both its muxer and
# its encoder; adts (AAC) and ogg (Vorbis) are copy-only and need only the
# muxer.
--enable-encoder=aac,libmp3lame,libopus,pcm_s16le,flac
--enable-muxer=mp3,pcm_s16le,flac,adts,ogg
--enable-libmp3lame
--enable-libopus

# Resampling for the device sample-rate ceilings, and every filter connect's
# own command lines can name. `volume` is the ReplayGain one (streamer.py and
# audio_analysis.py both build "-af volume=<gain>"), and its absence is what
# a missing filter looks like: the build succeeds and playback fails at the
# moment someone has ReplayGain on, with "Error parsing filterchain
# 'volume=0.9'".
--enable-swresample
--enable-filter=aresample,anull,aformat,volume

# One self-contained executable to copy into an image or an app bundle.
--disable-shared
--enable-static

# Autodetected extras, off by name rather than by absence. --disable-everything
# above covers components, not the host libraries and frameworks configure
# goes looking for on its own — and what it finds depends on what happens to
# be installed on the machine that built it. A macOS runner with Homebrew
# produced an ffmpeg linked against libX11, libxcb, libXau and libXdmcp from
# /opt/homebrew (measured 2026-09-08), none of which a listener's Mac has.
# X11 is for grabbing a screen, the Apple frameworks for hardware video —
# neither is anything an audio-only build has a use for.
--disable-xlib
--disable-libxcb
--disable-videotoolbox
--disable-audiotoolbox
--disable-coreimage
--disable-metal
--disable-sdl2
